"Towards a Science of AI Agent Reliability | Peter Kirgis (https://peterkirgis.github.io/research/reliability)\nciteturn32021view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://peterkirgis.github.io/research/reliability\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 27\nL0:   * cite0†Main L1:   * cite1†Research L2:   * cite2†Essays L3:   * cite3†Presentations L4:   * cite4†Miscellaneous L5:   * cite5†CV L6: \nL7: cite6†Image: Peter Kirgis L8: \nL9: ## Peter Kirgis\nL10: \nL11: Research Scientist at Princeton CITP\nL12: \nL13:   * pk7019@princeton.edu\nL14:   * cite7†GitHub†github.com L15:   * cite8†CV (PDF) L16: # Towards a Science of AI Agent Reliability\nL17: \nL18: February 21, 2026\nL19: AI agents are increasingly deployed to execute important tasks. While rising accuracy scores on standard benchmarks suggest rapid progress, many agents still continue to fail in practice. This discrepancy highlights a fundamental limitation of current evaluations: compressing agent behavior into a single success metric obscures critical operational flaws. Notably, it ignores whether agents behave consistently across runs, withstand perturbations, fail predictably, or have bounded error severity.\nL20: Grounded in safety-critical engineering, we provide a holistic performance profile by proposing twelve concrete metrics that decompose agent reliability along four key dimensions: consistency, robustness, predictability, and safety. Evaluating 14 models across two complementary benchmarks, we find that recent capability gains have only yielded small improvements in reliability.\nL21: By exposing these persistent limitations, our metrics complement traditional evaluations while offering tools for reasoning about how agents perform, degrade, and fail.\nL22: Recommended citation: Rabanser, S., Kapoor, S., Kirgis, P., Liu, K., Utpala, S., & Narayanan, A. (2026). Towards a Science of AI Agent Reliability. arXiv preprint arXiv:2602.16666.\nL23: \nL24: cite9†Paper†arxiv.org cite10†Website†hal.cs.princeton.edu L25: \nL26: © 2026 Peter Kirgis. Powered by cite11†Jekyll†jekyllrb.com .\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n[2602.16666] Towards a Science of AI Agent Reliability (https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.16666)\nciteturn32021view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.16666\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 164\nL0: cite0†Search cite1†Submit cite2†Donate†info.arxiv.org cite3†Log in L1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · cite4†Advanced search L5: \nL6: # Computer Science > Artificial Intelligence\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 18 Feb 2026 (cite5†v1 ), last revised 2 Jun 2026 (this version, v3)]\nL9: # Title:Towards a Science of AI Agent Reliability\nL10: \nL11: Authors:cite6†Stephan Rabanser , cite7†Sayash Kapoor , cite8†Peter Kirgis , cite9†Kangheng Liu , cite10†Saiteja Utpala , cite11†Arvind Narayanan L12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled Towards a Science of AI Agent Reliability, by Stephan Rabanser and 5 other authors\nL14: \nL15: cite12†View PDF cite13†HTML (experimental) L16: > Abstract:AI agents are increasingly deployed to execute important tasks. While rising accuracy scores on standard benchmarks suggest rapid progress, many agents still continue to fail in practice. This discrepancy highlights a fundamental limitation of current evaluations: compressing agent behavior into a single success metric obscures critical operational flaws. Notably, it ignores whether agents behave consistently across runs, withstand perturbations, fail predictably, or have bounded error severity.\nL17: Grounded in safety-critical engineering, we provide a holistic performance profile by proposing twelve concrete metrics that decompose agent reliability along four key dimensions: consistency, robustness, predictability, and safety. Evaluating 15 models across two complementary benchmarks, we find that recent capability gains have only yielded small improvements in reliability.\nL18: By exposing these persistent limitations, our metrics complement traditional evaluations while offering tools for reasoning about how agents perform, degrade, and fail.\nL19: Comments:  | Accepted at ICML 2026. Interactive dashboard available at: cite14†this https URL†hal.cs.princeton.edu L20: Subjects:  | Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Computers and Society (cs.CY); Machine Learning (cs.LG)\nL21: Cite as:  | cite15†arXiv:2602.16666 [cs.AI]\nL22:    | (or cite16†arXiv:2602.16666v3 [cs.AI] for this version)\nL23:    | cite17†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2602.16666†doi.org arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL24: ## Submission history\nL25: \nL26: From: Stephan Rabanser [cite18†view email ]\nL27: cite5†[v1] Wed, 18 Feb 2026 18:05:44 UTC (1,371 KB)\nL28: cite19†[v2] Mon, 23 Feb 2026 18:49:07 UTC (1,448 KB)\nL29: [v3] Tue, 2 Jun 2026 02:15:05 UTC (986 KB)\nL30: \nL31: Full-text links:\nL32: \nL33: ## Access Paper:\nL34: \nL35: View a PDF of the paper titled Towards a Science of AI Agent Reliability, by Stephan Rabanser and 5 other authors\nL36: \nL37:   * cite12†View PDF L38:   * cite13†HTML (experimental) L39:   * cite20†TeX Source L40: \nL41: cite21†view license†creativecommons.org L42: ### Current browse context:\nL43: \nL44: cs.AI\nL45: \nL46: cite22†< prev |   cite23†next > L47: \nL48: cite24†new | cite25†recent | cite26†2026-02 L49: \nL50: Change to browse by:\nL51: \nL52: cite27†cs L53: cite28†cs.CY L54: cite29†cs.LG L55: \nL56: ### References & Citations\nL57: \nL58:   * cite30†NASA ADS†ui.adsabs.harvard.edu L59:   * cite31†Google Scholar†scholar.google.com L60:   * cite32†Semantic Scholar†api.semanticscholar.org L61: \nL62: [Button: export BibTeX citation] Loadi